# Testing

- Use Django's built-in test runner and place tests in each app's `tests.py` or
  `tests/` package, following the existing structure. Do not add pytest or a type
  checker unless the project adopts it explicitly.
- Add regression tests for bug fixes and tests for changed behaviour. Cover
  meaningful success, invalid-input, and permission-denied cases rather than
  mirroring the implementation.
- Test model validation and constraints, forms, views, template output, and
  Wagtail page serving or editorial behaviour as relevant. Use Wagtail testing
  helpers where useful; exercise drafts/previews and publishing when affected.
- Use `django.test.TestCase` for database tests, `SimpleTestCase` when no database
  is needed, and `TransactionTestCase` when actual transaction behaviour matters.
- Keep tests isolated and deterministic. Mock external services at their boundary;
  do not make live network calls. Use focused test data and temporary media storage
  for upload tests, cleaning up files afterwards.
- Run targeted tests during development by passing an app or test label to
  `manage.py test`; run the full suite before completing backend changes.

# Formatting and required checks

Use the existing Ruff and djangofmt configuration in `pyproject.toml`. Fix lint
issues and format changed Python/templates with:

```shell
docker compose run --rm web uv run ruff check --fix .
docker compose run --rm web uv run ruff format .
docker compose run --rm web uv run djangofmt .
```

Inspect formatter changes and avoid unrelated formatting churn. Before completing
backend changes, run all of these and resolve failures caused by the change:

```shell
docker compose run --rm web uv lock --check
docker compose run --rm web uv run ruff check .
docker compose run --rm web uv run ruff format --check .
docker compose run --rm web uv run python scripts/check_django_templates.py
docker compose run --rm web uv run python manage.py check
docker compose run --rm web uv run python manage.py makemigrations --check --dry-run
docker compose run --rm web uv run python manage.py test
```

Documentation-only changes may use focused verification. If a required check
cannot run, report the command and blocker; do not claim it passed or weaken
checks to get a green result.

Install Git hooks on the host with `uv run pre-commit install`. Hooks use host UV;
they complement the container checks. Run `uv run pre-commit run --all-files`
when checking hooks. Pre-commit only checks files known to Git, so new files must
be intentionally staged to be included. Do not stage unrelated user changes.
